# Holdout content is never rendered as a diff.
#
# The read guard (.claude/settings.json, .claude/hooks/deny_sealed_history.py)
# stops a session *asking* for holdout content. It cannot stop git from
# volunteering it: `git show <merge>`, a PR page, `git log -p` over a range that
# happens to include these files, or a reviewer scrolling past. The isolated
# session's merge is precisely such a moment -- 35 answer-key files and 54
# questions arriving in one commit -- and at that point the build session is
# reading a diff for ordinary reasons.
#
# `-diff` marks these paths binary for diff purposes, so git prints
# "Binary files ... differ" instead of their contents. Nothing is encrypted and
# nothing is hidden from anyone determined: `git show <sha>:<path>` still works,
# which is what the hook is for. This closes the accidental path, not the
# deliberate one, and the accidental path is the one nobody is watching for.
eval/reference_sql/HO-*      -diff
eval/questions/holdout*      -diff

# The counts manifest is the one safe channel out of the isolated run (§5.2 of
# docs/ISOLATED_REFERENCE_RUN.md): counts, hashes and booleans, no values and no
# SQL. It is meant to be reviewed in a diff, so it keeps one.
eval/reference_sql/HO_MANIFEST.json  diff
